(; expect 1 ;)
(; Negative control, annotation_under_binder.dk two binders deep: x is annotated with T, but its
   position (the body of z => w => x, where K z w is expected) gives it the type U0, since K z w
   reduces to U0.  An instance need not respect the annotation: g (f (z => w => u0)), of type T,
   would rewrite to u0, of type U0.  The obligation through nested abstractions is made only at a
   constant-headed body, never at a pattern variable. ;)
U0 : Type.
T : Type.
D : Type.
u0 : U0.
def K : D -> D -> Type.
[z, w] K z w --> U0.
f : (z : D -> w : D -> K z w) -> T.
def g : T -> T.
[x : T] g (f (z => w => x)) --> x.
